# Final benchmark results, four models at n=50 (FREEZE #2, 2026-07-15)

All numbers script-computed from the canonical files (never hand-typed):
`backend/runs/ladder-v1/skill_scores.csv` (skill), `beliefs.csv` +
`belief_metrics.csv` (detection / knowing-doing), `oracle_refs.csv` (20-rep
oracle means). Regenerate every table with
`uv run python runs/results_tables.py` (from `backend/`; Tables 1–4) or
`bench.py report`. This file was regenerated 2026-07-15 from the 2026-07-11
run files (commit 932ce7a) after the deepseek/grok completion pass.

**Convention (settled at this freeze): belief tables are episode-pooled**
(every stress episode / stress week counts once, as printed by
`results_tables.py`). `report.md` / `belief_metrics.csv` use per-seed means;
the two agree within 0.03 everywhere (e.g. sonnet lag 0.39 vs 0.42). Any
table in the paper states which convention it uses; default is this file's.

Provenance: FAITHFUL prompt arm only. Four models, all 50 seeds each, via
OpenRouter: anthropic/claude-sonnet-5, openai/gpt-5.4,
deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro, x-ai/grok-4.5. Runs completed 2026-07-08 through
2026-07-11. gemini was dropped after a 1-seed smoke test (binding decision).

**Deepseek correction (must be disclosed if the pre-correction numbers were
ever shared):** the 2026-07-09 freeze reported deepseek on a 20-seed core
subset and excluded "6 pre-rationale traces". That was wrong about the
files: valid full-rationale re-runs existed on disk, but the belief grader
had graded stale overwritten files (its resume logic skipped re-runs). Fix:
156 stale `beliefs.csv` rows deleted (backup `beliefs.csv.bak`), the 20
genuinely missing deepseek seeds and 5 missing grok seeds were run, graded,
and rescored. Full-50 deepseek numbers differ drastically from the core-20
era (COMPOUND mean +0.24 vs +0.72; ALL −0.23 vs −0.24 with a very different
group profile) — never cite the core-20 numbers.

Traces: `backend/runs/ladder-v1/<model>/seedN-rich.chat.txt`, all validated
(26 weeks + EPISODE DONE). Belief labels: grader v2 prompt, same-week
alignment (`backend/runs/grade_beliefs.py`, gpt-5-mini, temperature 0).

Seed design: 50 seeds in three stress profiles — ISOLATED n=12, PERSISTENT
n=15, COMPOUND n=23. Sampling deliberately oversamples compound stress (the
regime the benchmark targets); group means are reported per group and never
pooled across groups without the per-group table alongside.

## Metric properties (state these wherever skill is reported)

- skill = (basestock − llm) / (basestock − oracle_mean). 0 = base-stock
  floor, 1 = fair-oracle mean. skill > 1 is legitimate (the oracle is fair,
  not optimal).
- Seed 11 has negative oracle headroom (basestock − oracle = −218): the
  floor already beats the fair oracle there, so skill is undefined. Excluded
  from all means as a metric property → flagship n=49. The paper must state
  this rule.
- Headroom across the 50 seeds: min −218, median 1211, max 2532. Thin
  headroom (< $700): seeds 36, 160, 164 — skill on these is noisy; flagged,
  not excluded.

## Table 1 — skill score

| model | ISOLATED | PERSISTENT | COMPOUND | ALL | neg seeds | beats floor |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| sonnet-5 | +0.54 (med +0.71, n=11) | +0.70 (med +0.61, n=15) | +0.33 (med +0.35, n=23) | +0.49 (med +0.45, n=49) | 9 | 40/50 |
| gpt-5.4 | +0.21 (med +0.07, n=11) | +0.55 (med +0.61, n=15) | +0.86 (med +0.73, n=23) | +0.62 (med +0.65, n=49) | 8 | 42/50 |
| deepseek-v4-pro | −0.62 (med −0.57, n=11) | −0.68 (med −0.26, n=15) | +0.24 (med +0.32, n=23) | −0.23 (med +0.15, n=49) | 22 | 28/50 |
| grok-4.5 | −0.14 (med −0.32, n=11) | −0.24 (med −0.12, n=15) | −0.06 (med +0.05, n=23) | −0.13 (med −0.08, n=49) | 28 | 21/50 |

"Beats floor" is denominator-free (llm cost < basestock cost, counted over
every seed run including seed 11 and thin-headroom seeds) — use it as the
robustness companion to skill, which is denominator-sensitive on thin seeds.
neg = scoreable seeds below the floor. Per-seed values: skill_scores.csv.

Shape notes (single run per cell):
- Two of four models end BELOW the symptom-blind base-stock floor overall.
- The old "gpt and sonnet swap ranks along the stress axis" claim is dead:
  sonnet's best group is PERSISTENT (+0.70, 0 negative seeds), not ISOLATED.
  The real shape is 3-vs-1: gpt, deepseek, and grok are all comparatively
  best on COMPOUND; sonnet is the lone exception (worst there; 7 of its 9
  negative seeds are COMPOUND).
- Deepseek's core-20 vs expansion-30 split is consistent (ALL mean −0.24 vs
  −0.23) — the expansion did not drag it down; the core-20-era story was an
  artifact of which seeds were graded/scored.
- Deepseek COMPOUND retains one extreme positive seed (99, skill 1.85);
  prefer the median when citing deepseek COMPOUND.

## Table 2 — detection (episode-pooled)

Episode = maximal run of consecutive weeks one factor is truly stressed
(283 episodes over the 50 seeds, per model). Lag in weeks from episode
start to the factor first appearing in the agent's stated rationale.

| model | episodes missed | detected | mean lag (detected) |
|---|---|---|---|
| sonnet-5 | 33/283 | 88% | 0.42 |
| gpt-5.4 | 35/283 | 88% | 0.40 |
| deepseek-v4-pro | 46/283 | 84% | 0.32 |
| grok-4.5 | 37/283 | 87% | 0.32 |

Detection is flat: 84–88% across a skill spread of +0.62 to −0.23, and the
two models that LOSE to the symptom-blind floor name factors slightly
FASTER than the two that beat it. Perception does not separate these
models; action does. This is the paper's headline dissociation.

## Table 3 — knowing-doing rate (pooled weeks)

Among weeks where the agent's rationale correctly named at least one truly
stressed factor: fraction that still ended in a stockout.

| model | ISOLATED | PERSISTENT | COMPOUND | all |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| sonnet-5 | 0.24 (n=72) | 0.43 (n=187) | 0.30 (n=256) | 0.34 (n=515) |
| gpt-5.4 | 0.08 (n=63) | 0.37 (n=188) | 0.22 (n=251) | 0.26 (n=502) |
| deepseek-v4-pro | 0.07 (n=60) | 0.40 (n=161) | 0.22 (n=248) | 0.26 (n=469) |
| grok-4.5 | 0.09 (n=69) | 0.34 (n=199) | 0.21 (n=276) | 0.24 (n=544) |

The rate peaks on PERSISTENT for all four models (0.34–0.43) and is lowest
on ISOLATED (0.07–0.24) — a uniform regularity at the belief level.

**KDR is ONE channel of acting-failure, not a ranking.** Overall KDR
rank-inverts with skill: sonnet (near-best skill) has the highest rate,
grok (worst skill) the lowest. The gap has two faces — under-response
(stockout despite correct diagnosis; what this table measures) and
over-response (premium-lever overspend / hoarding to avoid stockouts, which
a stockout-based rate cannot see). Grok and deepseek buy their low KDR with
over-response. Never present Table 3 as "who converts knowing into doing
best" without this framing. Behavioral evidence: TRACE-INSIGHTS-2026-07-10.md.

## Table 4 — severity confound check (report next to Table 3)

Stockout rate on truly-stressed weeks, split by whether the agent's
rationale correctly diagnosed the stress that week:

| model | diagnosed | undiagnosed |
|---|---|---|
| sonnet-5 | 0.34 (n=515) | 0.19 (n=162) |
| gpt-5.4 | 0.26 (n=502) | 0.14 (n=175) |
| deepseek-v4-pro | 0.26 (n=469) | 0.11 (n=208) |
| grok-4.5 | 0.24 (n=544) | 0.12 (n=133) |

Per-group split printed by `runs/results_tables.py`; the extreme case is
grok PERSISTENT: 0.34 diagnosed vs 0.03 undiagnosed. Diagnosed weeks stock
out MORE than undiagnosed ones, for every model and in every group. So the
knowing-doing rate is confounded by severity: weeks bad enough to be
noticed are also bad enough to stock out, and a stockout in week W may be
caused by an order placed weeks earlier. Interpret Table 3 as "correct
diagnosis does not guarantee shelf protection," NOT as "diagnosis causes
stockouts" or a week-level fault measure. The paper must carry this caveat,
and cross-model KDR comparisons stay soft.

## Interpretation constraints for the paper (from the trace audit)

Full evidence in `research/TRACE-INSIGHTS-2026-07-10.md` (adversarially
verified). Binding on any prose that uses these results:

- Failure taxonomy (quotable): sonnet over-doses REAL disruptions — in its
  9 negative seeds, air-expedite spend alone exceeds its entire excess over
  the floor, and ~90% of those air calls coincide with genuine disruption.
  gpt structurally under-uses levers — skips lock_freight in 87% of its
  freight-spike seeds and inspect_batch in 63% of its quality-reject seeds
  while naming the condition in its rationale.
- World-mechanic disclosures (not agent failures): (a) `contract_action=
  lapse` is a no-op on live contracts — once a second contract is signed
  the ~$4/week dual-source fee is unavoidable for everyone; (b) buy_audit /
  buy_briefing are near-deterministic ground-truth oracles by design —
  usage rate is the model variable, never reading accuracy.
- Batch-vs-drip: gpt is the confirmed large-batch outlier (~9 order-weeks
  of ~53 units); the other three are comparably dripper-like. No clean
  "closest to base-stock" ranking exists.
- The refuted-claims list in TRACE-INSIGHTS §5 is do-not-reintroduce.

## Grader-label validation (done 2026-07-08, on the 20-seed subset)

Two independent checks; second-grader data in
`backend/runs/ladder-v1/grader_agreement.csv`:

1. Stratified 30-row manual read (10 metric-relevant hits, 10 misses, 10
   calm-week labels) against raw trace rationales: all 10 hit-class
   intersections correct; 9/10 misses genuinely the model's (1 ambiguous,
   direction pessimistic for detection); calm-week labels ~50% over-labeled,
   a class the metrics exclude by construction.
2. Independent second grader (claude-haiku-4.5, identical prompt, 100 sampled
   weeks): exact-label agreement 43% — the task is ambiguous on borderline
   benign mentions — but agreement on the metric-relevant quantity
   (label ∩ true stress) is 89%.

The validation sampled the original 20-seed grading run; all later grading
(30 expansion seeds, deepseek regrade, grok) used the identical
prompt/model/temperature and is covered by the same argument, not by a
fresh sample.

